th='A foolish king and minister turn day into night; a wise guru saves his greedy disciple',
sum='In the Kingdom of Fools the king and his minister are idiots: they order everyone to sleep in the daytime and work at night, on pain of death; and everything – food or anything – costs the same price, one duddu. A guru and his disciple arrive; the guru feels it is dangerous to stay among fools, but the disciple stays because food is cheap and he grows fat. One night a thief breaks into a merchant’s house; the wall collapses and kills his brother. The thief complains to the king, who tries a chain of people: the merchant (owner of the wall), the bricklayer who built it (says a dancing girl distracted him), the dancing girl (says the goldsmith made her walk up and down for her jewels), the goldsmith (says the merchant’s father had ordered jewellery first) – and finally the merchant, as the son of the man responsible. The king orders the merchant to be impaled, but the stake is too big for the thin merchant, so they look for a fat man to impale – and catch the disciple. The disciple remembers his guru’s words (“This is a city of fools; don’t stay here”). The guru comes, whispers to the disciple, and both quarrel to be executed first, saying whoever dies on that stake at that auspicious moment will be reborn as a king (and the next as a minister). The foolish king and minister want that themselves, so they get impaled and die. The confused people request the guru and the disciple to become the new king and minister; they agree on condition that they can change all the old laws.',
te='మూర్ఖుల రాజ్యంలో రాజు, మంత్రి పగలు నిద్ర, రాత్రి పని అని ఆజ్ఞాపించారు; ప్రతి వస్తువుకు ఒకే ధర – ఒక దుడ్డు. గురువు వద్దన్నా శిష్యుడు చౌకైన ఆహారం కోసం అక్కడే ఉండి లావయ్యాడు. దొంగ సోదరుడు గోడ కూలి చనిపోతే రాజు వ్యాపారి–తాపీ మేస్త్రి–నర్తకి–కంసాలి… అంటూ తీర్పు ఇచ్చి, చివరికి శూలానికి సరిపోయే లావైన శిష్యుడిని ఉరి తీయబోయాడు. గురువు ఉపాయంతో “ఈ శుభ ముహూర్తంలో చనిపోయినవాడు మళ్ళీ రాజవుతాడు” అనగా మూర్ఖ రాజు, మంత్రి స్వయంగా శూలమెక్కి చనిపోయారు.',
